 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Marking",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "The can top carries a coloured band over the negative side, the lot number, a series code that depends on the case size, the rated capacitance in µF and the rated voltage in V."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Failure modes and rate",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "Failure rate is 0.5 %/1000 h at a 60 % reliability level (JIS C 5003). Random failures, mostly from thermal, electrical or mechanical stress during soldering or use, end as a short circuit; odorous gas may appear from seconds to minutes after such a fault, and a very large current through the short can make the part spark out and, at worst, ignite. Wear-out at end of life turns the electrolyte insulating and ends as an open circuit."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Where not to use it",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "Its leakage current rules it out of high-impedance, coupling and timing (time-constant) circuits and anything sensitive to leakage. Leakage can rise after soldering or other thermal and mechanical stress and falls back again with voltage applied, through self-healing. Capacitance and ESR may move within their specified limits during normal use."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Voltage",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Impact, transient and pulse voltages must stay at or below the rated voltage."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Inrush current",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "Rapid charge or discharge with an excessive rush current can short the part or raise its leakage. A protection circuit is applied when the rush current reaches 10 A on a part whose ten-fold allowable ripple current is under 10 A, or exceeds the ten-fold value on a part whose ten-fold ripple current is 10 A or more."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Case insulation",
   "page": 5,
   "text": "The resin sleeve is not an insulator, and the aluminum can has an undefined resistance to the cathode terminal; the can must be kept clear of both terminals and of copper on the board."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Soldering",
   "page": 6,
   "text": "Flow (wave) soldering of the surface-mount parts and dipping the body in molten solder are not allowed. Vapour-phase soldering conditions are to be agreed with the manufacturer. After soldering the part must not be tilted, twisted, struck or used as a handle for the board."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Cleaning and coating",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Cleaning by immersion or ultrasonics is limited to 2 minutes in a solution at 60 °C or below, with higher alcohol cleaners, CFC substitutes such as AK-225AES, or IPA; dry with hot air under the category upper temperature. Acetone must not be used in fixing or coating agents, and flux residue must be removed from between board and seal before coating."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Environment",
   "page": 4,
   "text": "Performance may be affected in liquids, direct sun, unshielded outdoor places, dust, condensation or water leakage, salt air, corrosive gases (Cl2, H2S, NH3, SO2, NOx), acid or alkali atmospheres, strong static or electromagnetic fields, heavy vibration or impact, low air pressure, next to hot or flammable parts, sealed in resin, or when flux is cleaned off with solvent, water or water-soluble cleaner."
  }
 ],
